    <!-- ============================================================ 8 -->
    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">08</span> Memory hierarchy &mdash; RDNA edition</h2>
      <p>
        Same layered story as CDNA, with consumer-silicon numbers. The
        absolute bandwidths are smaller across the board, but
        <strong>Infinity Cache (96 MB on the 7900 XTX; 64 MB on the R9700)</strong> is a
        uniquely RDNA tier &mdash; it sits between L2 and DRAM and
        dramatically widens the effective LLM-friendly working set.
      </p>

      <div class="distil-fig">
        <div class="fig-caption">fig &middot; memory hierarchy, 7900 XTX class</div>
        <pre class="ascii-fig mem-fig">
 tier             scope         cap          BW          lat
 ────             ─────         ───          ──          ───
 VGPRs            per CU        8 KB/wave    ~100 TB/s   0 c
 LDS              per WGP       ≤64 KB/WG   ~10 TB/s    ~20 c
 L1               per CU        32 KB        ~5 TB/s     ~50 c
 L2               per array     6 MB         ~3 TB/s     ~150 c
 Infinity Cache   device-wide   64–96 MB     ~17 TB/s    ~200 c   ← RDNA-only
 GDDR / sys RAM   device-wide   8–96+ GB     0.5–1.0     ~400 c
        </pre>
      </div>

      <p>
        The standard CDNA-deck rule still holds: <strong>every step down the
        ladder is at least an order of magnitude slower</strong>, so a kernel
        that touches the same byte twice should get the second touch from
        LDS/L1, not DRAM. The RDNA twist is that <strong>Infinity Cache
        makes the device-wide working set ~96 MB instead of ~6 MB of
        L2 on the 7900 XTX</strong> &mdash; a short-context KV slice can live
        entirely in IC, which helps <code>gfx1100</code> decode punch above
        its raw GDDR bandwidth tier.
      </p>
    </section>

